<!-- ================= 1. NEW & NOTEWORTHY ================= -->
<section id="new" class="cltp-sec" style="--sec:#E29C04;">
  <div class="cltp-wrap">
    <p class="cltp-kicker">The Fresh Stuff</p>
    <h2>New Restaurants in Charlotte: What Just Opened</h2>
    <div class="cltp-prose">
      <p>Charlotte&#8217;s restaurant scene moves fast — this block is where we track the new restaurants in Charlotte worth knowing about right now, updated as openings happen.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cltp-weekend-box">
      <span class="cltp-badge">Updated Regularly</span>
      <!-- ============================================================
           EDIT ZONE — NEW & NOTEWORTHY PICKS
           Swap the <li> items below as new spots open (keep 3–5).
           When a spoke post exists, link the restaurant name to it.
           ============================================================ -->
      <ul>
        <li><strong>flora</strong> (South End) — the Hamptons transplant everyone&#8217;s talking about opened July 15 in the old Eagle Food &amp; Beer Hall space on South Boulevard: seasonal New American from chef Benjamin McKinney, with a 27-seat bar at the center of the room. <a href="/discover-flora-restaurant-charlotte-nc-a-culinary-delight/">Our first look at flora</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>No Knife Chicken &amp; Waffles</strong> (700 Atando Ave.) — DaBaby&#8217;s chicken-and-waffles spot in north Charlotte opened September 7, 2026. <a href="/discover-dababy-restaurant-charlotte-a-culinary-adventure/">Our earlier coverage</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Uchi + Uchibā</strong> (Plaza Midwood) — James Beard winner Tyson Cole&#8217;s famed Japanese concept landed in March, instantly one of the toughest reservations in town.</li>
        <li><strong>Grey&#8217;s Diner</strong> (The Plaza) — chef Sam Diminich of Restaurant Constance doing elevated diner food, open since April.</li>
        <li><strong>Maman</strong> (three Charlotte cafés) — the cult NYC French bakery-café opened its first Carolinas location in Plaza Midwood (1711 Commonwealth Ave.) on May 20, then added South End (2000 South Blvd.) on June 25 and Eastover (523 Fenton Place) on July 29.</li>
      </ul>
      <!-- ============== END EDIT ZONE ============== -->
      <p style="margin:18px 0 0;"><a class="cltp-btn" style="background:var(--charcoal);color:#fff!important;" href="/">Today&#8217;s Charlotte Headlines →</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= 2. THE ESSENTIALS ================= -->
<section id="essentials" class="cltp-sec cltp-sec--band" style="--sec:#EE5565;">
  <div class="cltp-wrap">
    <p class="cltp-kicker">Start Here</p>
    <h2>The Essentials: Charlotte&#8217;s Best Restaurants</h2>
    <div class="cltp-prose">
      <p>If you only work through one list on this page, make it this one. These are the best restaurants in Charlotte, NC in our book — the rooms that define how this city eats, from a Michelin-starred tasting counter to a Vietnamese institution where regulars get remembered by their order.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cltp-rows">
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="counter">Counter‑ <span class="cltp-when">$$$$ · Wesley Heights</span></h3><p>Chef Sam Hart&#8217;s music-paired tasting menu made history in 2025 as Charlotte&#8217;s first Michelin-starred restaurant — it took a Green Star for sustainability, too. The city&#8217;s definitive special-occasion ticket.</p></div></div>
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="rada">Rada <span class="cltp-when">$$$ · Myers Park</span></h3><p>Seasonal Mediterranean in a dim, intimate room — and the only Charlotte restaurant named to the New York Times&#8217; 50 Best Restaurants in America. The signature dish is, improbably, a cabbage entrée. Order it anyway.</p></div></div>
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="haberdish">Haberdish <span class="cltp-when">$$ · NoDa</span></h3><p>Mill-town Southern cooking — crispy fried chicken, hush puppies, mac and cheese — with apothecary-style cocktails. The anchor of NoDa dining since 2016, and now Michelin-recommended.</p></div></div>
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="supperland">Supperland <span class="cltp-when">$$$ · Plaza Midwood</span></h3><p>Wood-fired steaks and Southern sides in a restored 1950s church that might be the prettiest dining room in the South. Bar director Colleen Hughes won Michelin&#8217;s Exceptional Cocktails Award.</p></div></div>
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="lang-van">Lang Van <span class="cltp-when">$ · Shamrock Dr</span></h3><p>Charlotte&#8217;s beloved Vietnamese institution since the &#8217;90s — pho, clay pots, a huge vegetarian menu, and hospitality so legendary Michelin gave it a Bib Gourmand in 2025.</p></div></div>
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="the-fig-tree">The Fig Tree <span class="cltp-when">$$$$ · Elizabeth</span></h3><p>Twenty-plus years of anniversaries and proposals in a 1913 Craftsman bungalow — rack of lamb, duck, and old-school service. Charlotte&#8217;s classic fine-dining room.</p></div></div>
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="restaurant-constance">Restaurant Constance <span class="cltp-when">$$$ · Wesley Heights</span></h3><p>Sam Diminich&#8217;s farm-driven New American spot, named for his daughter and built around local produce — a James Beard semifinalist nod and a Michelin recommendation in its first three years.</p></div></div>
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="merts-heart-and-soul">Mert&#8217;s Heart &amp; Soul <span class="cltp-when">$$ · Uptown</span></h3><p>Uptown&#8217;s soul-food cornerstone since 1998 — salmon cakes, shrimp and grits, soul rolls. Pre-game, post-church, and bring-your-out-of-town-guests approved.</p></div></div>
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="mama-ricottas">Mama Ricotta&#8217;s <span class="cltp-when">$$ · Midtown</span></h3><p>Family-style Italian-American since 1992 — penne alla vodka, wood-fired mozzarella — and the restaurant that launched a small Charlotte dining empire. A multi-generational ritual.</p></div></div>
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="midwood-smokehouse">Midwood Smokehouse <span class="cltp-when">$$ · Plaza Midwood</span></h3><p>The city&#8217;s consensus best barbecue: Texas-style brisket and burnt ends next to Eastern-NC chopped pork, all over hickory at the Central Avenue flagship.</p></div></div>
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="alexander-michaels">Alexander Michael&#8217;s <span class="cltp-when">$$ · Fourth Ward</span></h3><p>&#8220;Al Mike&#8217;s&#8221; has been pouring and serving in a Victorian-era former grocery since 1983 — the closest thing Uptown has to a time machine, Cajun chicken pasta included.</p></div></div>
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="omakase-by-prime-fish">Omakase by Prime Fish <span class="cltp-when">$$$$ · South CLT</span></h3><p>An intimate 15-course omakase counter that earned chef Robin Anthony Charlotte&#8217;s only 2026 James Beard semifinalist nod. The proof the new guard is here to stay.</p></div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= 3. RECENTLY CLOSED ================= -->
<section id="closed" class="cltp-sec" style="--sec:#5E5E69;">
  <div class="cltp-wrap">
    <p class="cltp-kicker">Save Yourself The Drive</p>
    <h2>Recently Closed — Don&#8217;t Make the Drive</h2>
    <div class="cltp-prose">
      <p>Charlotte saw more than 60 restaurant openings and more than 40 closings in 2025 alone, and most &#8220;best of Charlotte&#8221; lists haven&#8217;t kept up. Every place below is still being recommended somewhere on the internet. With one exception, noted below, you can&#8217;t go to any of them.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cltp-weekend-box cltp-closed-box">
      <span class="cltp-badge">Verified Closed</span>
      <!-- ============================================================
           EDIT ZONE — RECENTLY CLOSED
           Keep this to 6–9 items. Retire the oldest as new closures happen.
           Always keep one "moved, not closed" item in the last slot —
           readers find that the most useful line on the page.
           Re-verify via Google Business Profile FIRST (see build notes).
           ============================================================ -->
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Kindred</strong> (131 N Main St, Davidson) — <em>temporarily closed.</em> Closed since a fire on or about May 3, 2026. The Kindreds have not announced a reopening date, and as of mid-September 2026 the restaurant had not reopened. We&#8217;ll restore our recommendation when it does.</li>
        <li><strong><a href="https://cltdaily.com/good-food-on-montford-closing/">Good Food on Montford</a></strong> (Montford Drive) — closed Saturday, Aug. 29, 2026. Bruce Moffett did not renew the lease. The space is being renovated and will reopen as Bittersweet, a new concept from Lee Ann Dolcetto of Moffett Restaurant Group.</li>
        <li><strong>Fern, Flavors From The Garden</strong> (Dilworth) — closed May 2024 after 14 years. The space is now DŌZO Dilworth. Still on nearly every Charlotte vegan guide.</li>
        <li><strong>Bar-B-Q King</strong> (West Charlotte) — closed November 2025. A genuine institution.</li>
        <li><strong>Bulla Gastrobar</strong> (SouthPark) — closed December 2025.</li>
        <li><strong>Village Tavern</strong> (SouthPark) — closed by October 2024 after 35 years. The space is now Laurel Park.</li>
        <li><strong>Red at 28th</strong> (Dilworth) — closed January 2026. A relocation was promised; no new location has been announced.</li>
        <li><strong>33rd &amp; Main</strong> — closed May 2026. The owners cited rising food and fuel costs and said they&#8217;d rather close than shrink portions.</li>
        <li><strong>Soul Gastrolounge — moved, not closed.</strong> It left Plaza Midwood and reopened in NoDa in August 2025 at roughly double the size. Any guide still sending you to Central Avenue is out of date.</li>
      </ul>
      <!-- ============== END EDIT ZONE ============== -->
      <p style="margin:18px 0 0;font-size:.9rem;">Every restaurant recommended on this page was verified open as of October 1, 2026. We re-check monthly. If we&#8217;ve missed one, <a href="/contact/">tell us</a> — we&#8217;d rather hear it from you.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= 4. MICHELIN ================= -->
<section id="michelin" class="cltp-sec cltp-sec--band" style="--sec:#4C3957;">
  <div class="cltp-wrap">
    <p class="cltp-kicker">The Stars</p>
    <h2>Michelin Star Restaurants in Charlotte</h2>
    <div class="cltp-prose">
      <!-- ============================================================
           EDIT ZONE — MICHELIN
           Next selection announced Oct 21, 2026 (Nashville). Update same day.
           ============================================================ -->
      <p>Charlotte has exactly one. <strong>Counter‑</strong> in Wesley Heights received one Michelin star in the inaugural MICHELIN Guide American South, announced November 3, 2025 — the first Michelin star ever awarded to a restaurant in North Carolina. It took a Green Star for sustainability at the same time.</p>
      <p><strong>Lang Van</strong> holds Charlotte&#8217;s only Bib Gourmand, Michelin&#8217;s award for exceptional food at moderate prices.</p>
      <p>Eleven more Charlotte-area restaurants are listed as <strong>Recommended</strong>: Supperland, Customshop, Ever Andalo, Haberdish, Little Mama&#8217;s, Omakase Experience by Prime Fish, Prime Fish, Rada and Restaurant Constance, plus Hello Sailor in Cornelius and Kindred in Davidson. Supperland&#8217;s beverage director Colleen Hughes separately won the guide&#8217;s Exceptional Cocktails Award.</p>
      <p>The 2026 selection is announced <strong>October 21, 2026</strong> in Nashville. We&#8217;ll update this section the same day.</p>
      <p><strong>And one correction worth making:</strong> Charlotte has produced James Beard Award semifinalists in 2024, 2025 and 2026, and a finalist in 2023 — but the city has never produced a winner. If you see a Charlotte restaurant described as &#8220;James Beard Award-winning,&#8221; it&#8217;s wrong.</p>
      <div class="cltp-note">
        <p><strong>Worth knowing:</strong> North Carolina tourism offices paid Michelin $345,000 to have the state included in the guide, and the Charlotte Regional Visitors Authority separately spent $1.2 million — plus $2.5 million in state film incentives — to bring <em>Top Chef</em> to Charlotte and Greenville.</p>
        <p>That&#8217;s not a scandal. It&#8217;s how destination marketing works, and cities do it everywhere. But Charlotte&#8217;s arrival on the national food map was partly purchased, and you should know that when you read any list. Including this one.</p>
        <p>It also worked: searches for &#8220;best food in Charlotte&#8221; are up 52%, and food and beverage is now a quarter of visitor spending here.</p>
      </div>
      <!-- ============== END EDIT ZONE ============== -->
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= 5. BY NEIGHBORHOOD ================= -->
<section id="neighborhoods" class="cltp-sec" style="--sec:#6CD2CC;">
  <div class="cltp-wrap">
    <p class="cltp-kicker">Know Your Way Around</p>
    <h2>Charlotte Restaurants by Neighborhood</h2>
    <div class="cltp-prose">
      <p>Charlotte eats neighborhood by neighborhood — the right answer to &#8220;where should we eat?&#8221; depends on where you&#8217;re standing. Here’s how the map breaks down — and if you’re picking one to call home, our <a href="/living-in-charlotte-nc/">living in Charlotte guide</a> rates them for everyday life, not just dinner.</p>
    </div>

    <h3 id="uptown">Uptown &amp; Downtown Charlotte Restaurants</h3>
    <div class="cltp-prose"><p>Uptown (yes, locals call downtown Charlotte &#8220;Uptown&#8221;) is pre-theater dinners, power steakhouses, and <a href="/carolina-panthers/#eat">pre-game fuel on a Panthers Sunday</a> — most of it walkable from the stadiums and the Levine Center. Just northeast of it, <a href="https://cltdaily.com/optimist-hall-charlotte/">Optimist Hall</a> puts two dozen counters under one roof for the nights nobody can agree on a cuisine. If the game is the plan and dinner is the afterthought, here’s <a href="https://cltdaily.com/charlotte-sports-bars/">where to watch the game</a> — Charlotte’s sports bars, mapped by fan base.</p></div>
    <div class="cltp-rows">
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="church-and-union">Church and Union <span class="cltp-when">$$$ · N Tryon</span></h3><p>Top Chef alum Jamie Lynch&#8217;s modern-American room at 5th and Church Streets (formerly 5Church) — a reliable big-night pick with a strong weekend brunch.</p></div></div>
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="fin-and-fino">Fin &amp; Fino <span class="cltp-when">$$$ · Third Ward</span></h3><p>A &#8220;social seafood house&#8221; by the Levine Center — raw bar, champagne cart, and the best pre-show dinner near the Knight Theater.</p></div></div>
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="sea-level-nc">Sea Level NC <span class="cltp-when">$$ · E 5th St</span></h3><p>NC-sourced oysters (some from the restaurant&#8217;s own lease), caviar service, and a proper happy hour.</p></div></div>
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="la-belle-helene">La Belle Hélène <span class="cltp-when">$$$ · S Tryon</span></h3><p>A grand, mirrored Parisian brasserie — steak frites and escargot in the showiest room in Uptown.</p></div></div>
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3>Mert&#8217;s Heart &amp; Soul <span class="cltp-when">$$ · N College</span></h3><p>The soul-food institution — see <a href="#essentials">The Essentials</a>. Walkable to Spectrum Center before a Hornets game or show.</p></div></div>
    </div>

    <h3 id="south-end">South End Charlotte Restaurants</h3>
    <div class="cltp-prose"><p>South End is Charlotte&#8217;s densest eat-and-drink strip — Rail Trail patios, the Design District, and Atherton Mill, all strung along the Blue Line.</p></div>
    <div class="cltp-rows">
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3><a href="/discover-flora-restaurant-charlotte-nc-a-culinary-delight/">flora</a> <span class="cltp-when">$$$ · South Blvd</span></h3><p>The neighborhood&#8217;s newest headline: seasonal New American from a Hamptons restaurant family, open since mid-July 2026 — read our first look.</p></div></div>
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="o-ku">O-Ku <span class="cltp-when">$$$ · Design Center</span></h3><p>Upscale sushi and premium nigiri with a scene-y Design Center address — the dressed-up end of South End.</p></div></div>
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="superica">Superica <span class="cltp-when">$$ · Worthington</span></h3><p>Ford Fry&#8217;s Tex-Mex: mesquite-grilled fajitas, queso, and margaritas that explain the weekend waits.</p></div></div>
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="barcelona-wine-bar">Barcelona Wine Bar <span class="cltp-when">$$ · Atherton</span></h3><p>Spanish tapas and a 400-bottle wine list with one of the neighborhood&#8217;s best patios — the default South End date night.</p></div></div>
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="indaco">Indaco <span class="cltp-when">$$ · Atherton Mill</span></h3><p>Wood-fired pizzas, handmade pastas, and a spritz-friendly patio at Atherton Mill.</p></div></div>
    </div>

    <h3 id="noda">NoDa Restaurants</h3>
    <div class="cltp-prose"><p>The arts district eats like it looks: personality first. Haberdish is the anchor (see <a href="#essentials">The Essentials</a>) — here&#8217;s the rest of the North Davidson crawl.</p></div>
    <div class="cltp-rows">
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="the-goodyear-house">The Goodyear House <span class="cltp-when">$$ · N Davidson</span></h3><p>Elevated neighborhood comfort food in a renovated 1900s mill house — the smash burger is one of the city&#8217;s best — with a big patio.</p></div></div>
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="soul-gastrolounge">Soul Gastrolounge <span class="cltp-when">$$ · The Pass</span></h3><p>The Plaza Midwood legend moved to NoDa in 2025 at double the size — same globally inspired tapas, same famous pork-belly tacos with watermelon cubes.</p></div></div>
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="cabo-fish-taco">Cabo Fish Taco <span class="cltp-when">$$ · 35th &amp; Davidson</span></h3><p>Baja-style blackened mahi tacos and prime patio people-watching at NoDa&#8217;s main corner since 2003.</p></div></div>
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="ever-andalo">Ever Andalo <span class="cltp-when">$$$ · N Davidson</span></h3><p>Jeff Tonidandel and Jamie Brown&#8217;s Northern Italian room — handmade pasta, a proper amaro list, and the most grown-up dinner in NoDa. Michelin-recommended.</p></div></div>
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="benny-pennellos">Benny Pennello&#8217;s <span class="cltp-when">$ · N Davidson</span></h3><p>Absurd 28-inch pizzas by the giant slice — the pre-show and post-show fuel of the NoDa music crawl.</p></div></div>
    </div>

    <h3 id="plaza-midwood">Plaza Midwood Restaurants</h3>
    <div class="cltp-prose"><p>Between Supperland and Midwood Smokehouse (both in <a href="#essentials">The Essentials</a>), Plaza Midwood might be Charlotte&#8217;s best eating neighborhood per square block. It&#8217;s also where the national names keep landing.</p></div>
    <div class="cltp-rows">
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="uchi-uchiba">Uchi + Uchibā <span class="cltp-when">$$$$ · Central Ave</span></h3><p>Tyson Cole&#8217;s nationally famous Japanese concept arrived in 2026 — sushi worth planning a week around, if you can get the reservation.</p></div></div>
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="dish">Dish <span class="cltp-when">$$ · Thomas Ave</span></h3><p>Southern comfort food since 2002 — country-fried steak, fried green tomatoes, and a deviled egg with every entrée.</p></div></div>
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="the-diamond">The Diamond <span class="cltp-when">$ · Commonwealth</span></h3><p>A neighborhood diner running since 1945 — fried pickles, burgers, Greek mezze, breakfast anytime.</p></div></div>
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="moo-and-brew">Moo &amp; Brew <span class="cltp-when">$ · Central Ave</span></h3><p>Craft burgers and a deep beer list — ten years a Plaza Midwood staple and still the casual default.</p></div></div>
    </div>

    <h3 id="ballantyne">Ballantyne &amp; South Charlotte Restaurants</h3>
<div class="cltp-prose"><p>Ballantyne is really several separate places, and which one you are heading to changes where you should eat. Our full guide to <a href="https://cltdaily.com/ballantyne-restaurants/">Ballantyne restaurants</a> covers all of them &mdash; The Bowl, Ballantyne Village, Waverly, Rea Farms and the shopping centres people fold in with them.</p></div>
    <div class="cltp-prose"><p>South Charlotte&#8217;s dining has grown up alongside Ballantyne itself — hotel fine dining, rooftop views, and the area&#8217;s best family diner.</p></div>
    <div class="cltp-rows">
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="gallery-restaurant">Gallery Restaurant <span class="cltp-when">$$$ · The Ballantyne</span></h3><p>&#8220;The art of steak&#8221; inside The Ballantyne hotel — fine-dining steaks and contemporary Southern plates for the area&#8217;s special occasions.</p></div></div>
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="hestia-rooftop">Hestia Rooftop <span class="cltp-when">$$$ · Ballantyne Village</span></h3><p>Modern Asian-fusion on the 16th floor with skyline-to-treeline views — south Charlotte&#8217;s scene-y dinner.</p></div></div>
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="the-porters-house">The Porter&#8217;s House <span class="cltp-when">$$$ · Waverly</span></h3><p>A modern chophouse up Providence Road in Waverly — dry-aged steaks, raw bar, and a serious bourbon program.</p></div></div>
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="little-mamas">Little Mama&#8217;s <span class="cltp-when">$$ · SouthPark</span></h3><p>Wood-fired Italian-American from the Mama Ricotta&#8217;s family — bright room, blistered pizzas, and a Michelin recommendation.</p></div></div>
      <div class="cltp-row"><span class="cltp-row-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="cltp-row-body"><h3 id="big-view-diner">Big View Diner <span class="cltp-when">$ · Lancaster Hwy</span></h3><p>Scratch-made all-day diner classics — hot honey chicken and waffles, huge breakfasts — and the south-Charlotte family default.</p></div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

